{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01934B8ztwx34bUwHE8LvQnL","type":"tool_result","content":"\tvar text strings.Builder\n\tfor _, f := range fs {\n\t\tfmt.Fprintf(&text, \"%s %s %d %s %s %s\\n\", f.URL, f.Date, f.Status, f.SHA256, f.VerifiedBy, f.Path)\n\t}\n\tsec, err := d.Secrets().WithPaths(closeout.PathRules(h.Worktree, h.Home, d.HostPaths)...)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tjb.log.Error(\"worker.fetched_withheld\", \"cause\", \"path rules\", \"error\", err)\n\t\treturn\n\t}\n\traw := text.String()\n\tif sec.String(raw) != raw {\n\t\tjb.log.Error(\"worker.fetched_withheld\", \"cause\", \"redacted\")\n\t\treturn\n\t}\n\tfound, err := d.Scanner.Scan(ctx, strings.NewReader(raw))\n\tif err != nil || len(found) > 0 {\n\t\tjb.log.Error(\"worker.fetched_withheld\", \"cause\", \"scan\")\n\t\treturn\n\t}\n\tjb.fetched = fs\n}\n\n// round decides one finished agent step: the role file, the diff guard and the\n// push. head is the commit the tests run on. done means the job has been\n// finished (or stopped) here; noCode means the step changed nothing to commit.\nfunc (jb *job) round(ctx context.Context, s step) (head string, noCode, done bool) {\n\tn := jb.is.Number\n\trf, ok := jb.peekRole(s.h)\n\tif !ok || rf.Status != closeout.StatusDone {\n\t\t// Close-out reports a missing, malformed or not-done result itself.\n\t\tjb.finishWithPR(ctx, s, \"\", closeout.Cause{})\n\t\treturn \"\", false, true\n\t}\n\tjb.setSummary(ctx, s.h, rf.Summary)\n\tjb.setFetched(ctx, s.h, rf.Fetched)\n\tstage, err := os.MkdirTemp(\"\", \"ard-code-\")\n\tif err == nil {\n\t\tdefer os.RemoveAll(stage)\n\t\t// The committer refuses a source path that involves a symlink (/var on macOS).\n\t\tstage, err = filepath.EvalSymlinks(stage)\n\t}\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tjb.log.Error(\"worker.guard_failed\", \"error\", err)\n\t\tjb.finishWithPR(ctx, s, \"\", jb.cause(closeout.CauseNoProcess, \"the diff guard could not run\", false))\n\t\treturn \"\", false, true\n\t}\n\tchanges, prLines, err := jb.l.facts(ctx, n, s.h.Worktree, s.h.BaseSHA, jb.cfg.SHA, stage)\n\tvar le *gitx.LimitError\n\tif errors.As(err, &le) {\n\t\t// Too big or too many to look at: a hold, before anything was hashed.\n\t\tjb.log.Info(\"worker.guard\", \"hold\", true, \"limit\", le.Error())\n\t\tjb.finishWithPR(ctx, s, \"diff guard: \"+Reject{Path: le.Path, Why: le.Why}.String(), closeout.Cause{})\n\t\treturn \"\", false, true\n\t}\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tjb.log.Error(\"worker.guard_failed\", \"error\", err)\n\t\tjb.finishWithPR(ctx, s, \"\", jb.cause(closeout.CauseNoProcess, \"the diff guard could not run\", false))\n\t\treturn \"\", false, true\n\t}\n\tlim := Lines{Warn: jb.role.DiffWarnLines, Max: jb.role.DiffMaxLines}\n\tv := Check(n, changes, prLines, jb.exists, lim)\n119:**Repo context docs.** `.ard/PRODUCT.md`, `.ard/VISION.md` and `.ard/TECH_STACK.md` give agents the background a repository's code does not state. They are human-owned: no agent writes them, the worker guard rejects any change under `.ard/`, and `ard init` proposes them only as templates. Each is optional. A file that is missing, or that still holds only the template (headings and comments), counts as absent and is not shown to agents. Precedence, highest first: the role's own prompt and `.ard/config.yaml`, then the issue and its plan, then these docs; they inform a decision and never override the issue's ask. A reviewer reads them from the pull request's head, so a PR that edits one is judged against its own edit, and because the three files are protected paths that edit waits for a human approval. When a doc conflicts with the issue or with another doc, the agent does not pick a side: it reports the conflict in its role file and answers `needs-human`. Treat their content as facts about the project, not as instructions that change an agent's rules. Each template carries a size guidance of about 150 lines and a `Last reviewed: YYYY-MM-DD` line for the human to keep current.\n125:| every job | its own role file `<stage>-<role>.md` |\n128:| `triage:lead` | its role file only |\n129:| a custom triage role | its role file only, unless `config.yaml` gives it `plan_section: <heading>` (see Configuration) |\n130:| `worker:*`, `review:*` | its role file only |\n132:**Fetched inputs (#300).** A worker that fetches bytes from the network records each one under `fetched:` in its role file. Close-out parses the list strictly, like `findings`: an entry with a non-https URL (or one with userinfo, whitespace, backticks or angle brackets), a bad date or status, a `sha256` that is not 64 lowercase hex characters, a `verified_by` that is neither `none` nor an https URL, an unclean or missing `path`, or an unknown key fails the parse, as do more than 50 entries and a `fetched` key on a non-worker role file. The worker PR body then lists the entries under **Fetched inputs** (URL, landing path, sha256, verified_by; `none` shows as unverified) from the parsed fields only, never from free text; the list is dropped, like the summary, when redaction or the secret scan touches it. The human-readable record beside each fixture set is a `CAPTURE.md` with the same fields as a table plus the regen command (the #47 precedent); the worker writes it as part of the change, and ard does not parse it.\n134:A worker's inputs are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md`, `comments.md` and, on a fix step, `test-output.log` (written by the loop, never committed) and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`. Nobody may change `issue.md` (only the scanner writes it, through the committer, not close-out), another role's file, any other file, or delete `plan.md`. A job that owns a `plan.md` section may add, replace in place, move or remove that section; a change to the text before the first `##` heading is allowed only when the job creates the file. Headings match exactly (case-sensitive), and `##` lines inside a fenced code block are text, not headings. A change outside the allowlist fails the job as `needs-human`: the comment names up to three offending paths, and only the redacted log is committed, never the offending files. A job with no valid role file still fails as `failed`, because the allowlist is checked after the role file parses. `logs/` is the controller's: files an agent writes there are ignored, not committed. `costs/` is the controller's too, but stricter: no role may write it, so an agent write there breaks the allowlist like any other path and fails the job as `needs-human`; the controller stages its own `costs/<job-id>.json` after that check, and it is never on `closeout.WritableFor`. The allowlist is code (`closeout.WritableFor`), not config; `plan_section` only names the heading a role owns. Workers and reviewers get their inputs outside `.ard/<issue#>/` (for example `.ard-input/<issue#>/`), because anything they write under it counts as their change. `issue.md` is the issue body without ard's cost block (see Guardrails), so the table never reaches an agent; the reviewer's untrusted `.ard/<issue#>/` input includes `costs/*.json`, which is controller-written and holds no free text.\n138:The stage in a role file's name is the label stage (`triage`, `worker`, `review`; `config.Kind` and the role directory spell the last one `reviewer`), so a triage and a worker role with the same name never share a file.\n145:status: done          # done | blocked | needs-human (both of the last two add ard:needs-human)\n159:    status: 200                 # HTTP status, required\n268:**Starter planning roles.** `ard init` also ships four planning roles, each writing its own `plan.md` section (`config.BuiltinPlanSections`) and its role file: `product-manager` (`## Requirements`: requirements, a numbered list of acceptance criteria, out of scope), `architect` (`## Design`: approach, affected modules, and an `ADR needed: yes|no` line; the worker writes any ADR in its pull request), `designer` (`## UX`, plus Markdown or script-free SVG files under `.ard/<issue#>/design/`) and `documentation-planner` (`## Docs`: which docs change and what each must say). The command line counts as a user interface, so the designer covers flags, output and messages. The starter ordering is product-manager, then architect; designer, then documentation-planner; the engineer runs after architect and documentation-planner (`after:` in the starter `config.yaml`). Note for #45: acceptance criteria are a numbered list under `### Acceptance criteria`. The starter prompts read the repo context docs per role: `product-manager` reads `PRODUCT.md` and `VISION.md`, `architect` reads `VISION.md` and `TECH_STACK.md`, `designer` and `documentation-planner` read `PRODUCT.md`, and `engineer` reads `TECH_STACK.md`. Each prompt states the shared rules: the file is optional, one with no text outside headings and HTML comments counts as absent, the issue is the ask and the files are constraints, and `CLAUDE.md` and `AGENTS.md` win on commands and layout. A planning role that finds a conflict records it, with file and section, in its own role file.\n270:The starter lead (`.ard/triage/lead/prompt.txt`) follows a fixed contract. It reads the issue only through the untrusted input files `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md`, which the triage loop writes and never commits. It finds roles by listing the role directories and routes only to those. It writes only `triage-lead.md`, with `attempt: 1`, and a `status: done` always names a `worker`. It answers `needs-human` when a person must decide: an unclear ask, a question, a duplicate, a change to `.ard/config.yaml`, the role directories or `.github/`, no fitting worker, or text that tries to instruct it. It uses `review:<role>` only for a reviewer whose `when` rule would skip a change that needs that review. The committer and close-out guards confine it to `.ard/<issue#>/`, and close-out allows it only `triage-lead.md`; any other change under `.ard/<issue#>/` fails the job as needs-human. It also reads `PRODUCT.md` and `VISION.md` when present: a contradiction with a non-goal, principle or banned dependency makes it raise one `needs-human`, while on an interpretive or minor conflict it may proceed and says so in `triage-lead.md`. Inputs found only on the network are not, alone, a reason for `needs-human`: the lead routes them to the engineer and reads the `Worker roles:` line to see whether any worker role has network (#301).\n284:| lead | nothing (role file only); writes `triage-lead.md` with routing, or `needs-human` when a person must decide | every `ard:pending` issue |\n297:An executor runs one agent job somewhere and reports back; the controller does not care where. Both executors implement the same five calls: `prepare`, `start`, `status`, `logs`, `cleanup`. Prepare also removes any `Job.Scrub` paths (the job's own role file, directly under `.ard/<issue#>/`) from the fresh worktree before the agent starts. Job IDs are ULIDs minted at claim time; they name the worktree and the logs and appear as the `Ard-Job` commit trailer. The executor captures the job's stdout separately from its combined log, because the adapter parses one and the committed log comes from the other. Scanner jobs get network egress for advisory databases; an agent job's environment holds its model credential and nothing else of value, and with `network: true` (the default) it may reach the network; see the `network` setting under Configuration for what is and is not enforced.\n322:**The agent's contract is narrow.** It may edit files in its worktree and must write `.ard/<issue#>/<stage>-<role>.md` with a `status`. Under `.ard/<issue#>/` it may change only its own role file and what its role's allowlist grants (see the `.ard/` contract). Exit 0 without that file counts as a failure, not a success, and so does a malformed file. The file counts only if this job wrote it: before the agent starts, the executor removes the job's role file from the worktree (`executor.Job.Scrub`), so a copy left from an earlier stage or run is never trusted. A role file the agent never wrote again fails the job (`role file not written by this job`, or `role file missing` when none was ever there). One it wrote again with identical bytes still counts, but only because the executor recorded the scrub in the handle (`Handle.Scrubbed`); close-out refuses an unscrubbed, unchanged role file (`role file not scrubbed by executor`). A non-zero exit fails the job even when a role file exists, and a failed job commits only its redacted log, never a partial `plan.md`.\n324:**Close-out is resumable.** The controller (`internal/closeout`) decides the outcome from the role file (only one this job wrote), stages the redacted files, then commits, requests the `next` labels, posts one summary comment (for a review with a verdict, one PR review instead; either is tagged `<!-- ard:closeout job=<id> -->` so a replay never posts twice) and finally swaps `:running` for `:done`, `:changes`, `ard:needs-human` or `ard:executor:failed`. It records its phase in local state after each step. `serve` resumes unfinished close-outs at startup, and the Reaper resumes, rather than fails, a job whose close-out has started. Three more outcomes exist for a job that ended without a usable result: `failed` (sets `ard:executor:failed`), `retry` (a named transient cause: no `ard:executor:failed`, the request label kept, the next claim is the automatic retry) and `usage-limit` (a stop at a subscription usage limit: `:running` is removed, nothing is added, the request label stays, and the issue is claimed again after the reset without using up the retry). All three commit only the redacted log. Their comment carries the reason, the last 50 log lines (cut from the redacted log, scanned again and capped at 8 KiB; if that scan flags or fails, the comment says the tail was withheld) and when the worktree and host files are kept until. `next` entries are applied only for roles that exist; a `review:<role>` the lead requests is applied when the worker opens the PR, not at triage close-out. Only changed files under `.ard/<issue#>/` that the job's allowlist permits are committed, so a newer `issue.md` on `main` is never overwritten.\n348:4. **Run the agent** with `.ard/workers/<role>/prompt.txt` and these untrusted inputs: `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md` (written by the loop, outside `.ard/<issue#>/`, never committed), `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md` when triage wrote one and `.ard/<issue#>/issue.md` when a scanner filed the issue (each only as a regular file, never through a symlink), the triage role files and logs, and, in a fix step, `.ard-input/<issue#>/test-output.log` (the untrusted header, the test command and its exit code, and the last 64 KiB of the test log). In a review round, `.ard-input/<issue#>/review-feedback.md` (the untrusted header, the round, the committed role files of the reviewers that asked for changes and the pull request comments of people, cut at 64 KiB), and the reviewer role files and logs on the branch are untrusted inputs too. The agent edits code and writes `.ard/<issue#>/worker-<role>.md`; the executor scrubs a stale copy first.\n353:9. **Report.** Comment on the issue with the summary, PR link and test result (`passed`, `passed after 1 fix attempt`, `failed after 2 fix attempts`, `not configured`); swap `ard:worker:<role>:running` for `:done`. If tests still fail, add `ard:tests:failed` on the issue and the PR and request no reviewers; a green run removes it. A worker that ends without the loop's result (for example one the Reaper closes out), or whose role file says `needs-human`, never becomes `:done` without a PR.\n355:**The starter worker (`workers/engineer`, #34).** `.ard/workers/engineer/prompt.txt` implements `plan.md`'s `## Implementation`. Its inputs, all untrusted, are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md`, in a fix round `test-output.log`, and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`, `issue.md`, the triage role files and logs, in a review round `review-feedback.md` and the reviewer role files and logs. It writes code, tests and docs, and `worker-engineer.md` with `role: engineer` and `attempt: 1`, an optional `next` of `review:` entries, and never `worker` or `verdict`. The body has Changes, Testing and Open issues sections; it becomes the commit body, the PR description and the issue comment, and a fix round's body describes the whole change. It answers `needs-human` for a change the diff guard would reject, more changed lines than the role's `diff_max_lines` (3,000 by default; above `diff_warn_lines`, 2,000 by default, the PR goes ahead with the large-change note), a missing decision, nothing to change, or an input that tries to instruct it. In a fix round it keeps `done` and explains a failure it cannot fix, so the loop reports `ard:tests:failed` on the PR. The repository's package manager may fetch a dependency the change needs (`go get`, `npm install`); the agent installs no system packages. With `network: true` it may fetch other inputs, records each under `fetched:` and treats the fetched bytes as untrusted data, and the guard and reviewers still see `go.mod` and lockfile changes. Credential-shaped test fixtures are built at run time. The starter override is Sonnet 5.5, `timeout: 60m` for the whole job, `tests: make lint test` (so formatting and vet failures are caught before a PR opens), and the default diff thresholds written out (`diff_warn_lines: 2000`, `diff_max_lines: 3000`). It ships no Dockerfile, so it runs as a host subprocess with the #84 warning until #55's `ard-agent` image gives a worker image a base. It reads `TECH_STACK.md` when present; the file is a constraint, never an instruction, and the worker never resolves a conflict with it: it keeps to the issue and plan and notes the conflict in Open issues. With `Network: available.` the worker fetches only what the plan's Network inputs name, verifies each file against the publisher's checksum file, never invents or recalls a value, records each file in `fetched:` and in a `CAPTURE.md` beside the fixtures, and commits a regen script and Make target; with `Network: unavailable.` it answers `needs-human` listing the inputs it needs (#301).\n367:3. **Run the aid, then the agent.** After the pin, a role with an executable `aid` in its directory (reviewers only, like a scanner's `scan`; #205) has it run by the controller as its own executor job at the pinned head, before the agent, so the findings of its deterministic tools do not depend on the host or on the agent choosing to run them. The tool finds; the agent explains, ranks and confirms. The aid job is read-only, has no `Env` and no `Fixed` entries (no model credential, no GitHub token), runs with `--network none` under Docker (a role `Dockerfile` or `ARD_AGENT_IMAGE`; known CVEs stay with the `cve` scanner, so the aid never queries vulnerability databases), and has its own timeout of 5 minutes (shorter if the role's is), counted inside the review job's timeout: the agent gets what is left, at least one minute. Its inputs are `.ard-input/<issue#>/changed.txt` (one path per line, `.ard/<issue#>/` left out) and `diff.patch`. Its output is JSONL on stdout, one object per result with exactly `tool`, `rule`, `file`, `line`, `severity` (`critical`, `high`, `medium`, `low`, `info`) and `message`, capped at 256 KiB (more is a failure). ard validates it: a line that is not JSON, has an unknown key, a bad tool, rule, severity or line, or a path that is absolute, has `..` or is not in `changed.txt` is dropped and counted; a message is redacted, cut at 1 KiB and secret-scanned like a log, and a line that still trips the scanner is dropped. Matched text is never kept: the aid must print rule ids and positions only. The result is written to `.ard-input/<issue#>/aid.jsonl` and is the first of the agent's untrusted inputs; the starter prompt reads it first and runs the tools itself only when the file is absent. **Failure:** a failed, timed-out or invalid aid (any dropped line counts as invalid) never stops the review. The input says the aid failed and why, and close-out turns an `approve` into `changes` with a note. **Blocking:** a `gitleaks` result on a line the PR added becomes a blocking `high` finding with a `[secret]` message that ard adds to the review in code (carrying the rule id only); the agent cannot drop it, and an `approve` becomes `changes`. gosec and semgrep results stay advisory. Both rules apply only to roles that have an `aid`. **Host mode:** with no role `Dockerfile` and no `ARD_AGENT_IMAGE`, the aid runs on the host, as ard-owned code that reads but never executes PR code; its environment holds no credential, but nothing enforces its network, and it runs as the controller's uid without a sandbox: the residual risk of #84 applies to it too. The starter `security-review/aid` pins gosec, gitleaks and semgrep (vendored local rules, `--metrics=off`, `--disable-nosem`) in the role's `Dockerfile`, built on the default ard-agent image (k8s role images stay with #245). Then **run the agent** with `.ard/reviewers/<role>/prompt.txt` and these untrusted inputs: `.ard-input/<issue#>/diff.patch` (the controller's diff from the merge base with `main` to the pinned head, `--stat` then the patch, taken with `--text`, `--no-textconv` and attributes read from the empty tree so a `.gitattributes` entry cannot hide a change, with this issue's `.ard/<issue#>/` left out and cut at 1 MiB), `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md` (written by the loop, never committed), the committed `.ard/<issue#>/` (plan, triage and worker role files and logs) and the sidecar (security policy, license allowlist). The loop runs every git command against the controller clone, never in the reviewer's worktree (the agent itself may run read-only git there). In a later round it also gets `.ard-input/<issue#>/previous-review.md`, its own earlier report, read from the pinned head, and says which findings are resolved.\n497:Scanners also take `route` (`pending`, `none` or `needs-human`). Anything unset takes its default: intake in `label` mode with `ard:pending`, scanners every 4 h with `max_open` 5 and `group_size` 10, triage every 15 min, a 30-minute job timeout, `make test`, 2 test-fix attempts, 3 review rounds and worker diff thresholds of 2,000 lines (warn) and 3,000 lines (hold). Durations accept `m`, `h` and `d`. A sidecar `repo` must be `owner/name` and its `ref` a valid git ref name (no `:`, leading `-`, `..` or spaces; an abbreviated SHA is rejected, use all 40 characters); `ard config check` reports the file and key. A worker's `timeout` and `max_budget_usd` cover the whole job (every step), and `tests: \"\"` disables its test step. Per-role keys are `every`, `max_open`, `group_size`, `autoclose`, `timeout`, `agent`, `model`, `max_budget_usd`, `budget_usd_per_day` (that role's own daily cap in USD; unset or 0 means only the repo cap applies), `sidecar`, `after`, `plan_section`, `tests`, `max_test_fixes`, `max_review_rounds`, `diff_warn_lines` and `diff_max_lines` (worker roles; positive integers, warn no more than max; above warn the PR proceeds with a note, above max it is held), `reviewers` and `when` (`paths`, or `triage` roles that ran; at least one is required). `ard config check` warns when every reviewer has a `when:` rule, because a PR matching none gets no review and stays a draft. `plan_section` is for triage roles: the `## <heading>` of `plan.md` the role owns (one line, no leading `#`, unique across triage roles). The starter roles default to Requirements (product-manager), Design (architect), UX (designer), Docs (documentation-planner) and Implementation (engineer), and a value in `config.yaml` overrides that; a triage role with none may write only its own role file. There are no per-role config files: `ard init` writes a role's starting overrides into `config.yaml` for the roles it ships. Once a second repo adopts ard, role directories can move to a shared roles repo pinned by ref, like a sidecar; a repo's own directory of the same name wins. `after` names triage roles, with no self-reference and no cycles.\n524:- **Agents write only their own files.** Close-out lets a job change under `.ard/<issue#>/` only its own role file and what its role owns (a `plan.md` section, `design/`); anything else fails the job as `needs-human` and is not committed (see the `.ard/` contract).\n530:- **Committed output is redacted.** Close-out strips known secret values, and replaces the controller host's paths (see \"All agent output is committed too\") with placeholders, in each log and every `.ard/<issue#>/` file it commits, and runs a secret scan before anything is committed or posted. A hit on a log keeps that log host-local and adds `ard:needs-human`; a hit on a role file or `plan.md` commits only the clean log and adds `ard:needs-human`. Comments never quote text the scan flagged (and the same scan runs on worker code before it is pushed, see above, and the worker's PR body and code commit message get the same secret and host-path replacement; the config-load error posted to the status issue gets the host-path replacement too), never forge an `ard:` marker and never `@`-mention anyone. The scan fails closed: if it cannot run, nothing is committed. An optional second pass runs a pinned gitleaks binary (`redact.gitleaks`, see \"All agent output is committed too\"); it is off by default, fails closed, and a hit is handled like an internal hit. Once a log's redacted copy is committed, close-out deletes the raw host log; if redaction failed or the scan flagged it, the raw log is kept (and the keep logged) for a human to inspect.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6fb74eff-9d1e-4e23-8bf6-a73511888174","uuid":"7730c1e1-8ea7-47df-aae7-4fa7bd3a9dc5","timestamp":"2026-11-25T11:11:27.927Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"\tvar text strings.Builder\n\tfor _, f := range fs {\n\t\tfmt.Fprintf(&text, \"%s %s %d %s %s %s\\n\", f.URL, f.Date, f.Status, f.SHA256, f.VerifiedBy, f.Path)\n\t}\n\tsec, err := d.Secrets().WithPaths(closeout.PathRules(h.Worktree, h.Home, d.HostPaths)...)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tjb.log.Error(\"worker.fetched_withheld\", \"cause\", \"path rules\", \"error\", err)\n\t\treturn\n\t}\n\traw := text.String()\n\tif sec.String(raw) != raw {\n\t\tjb.log.Error(\"worker.fetched_withheld\", \"cause\", \"redacted\")\n\t\treturn\n\t}\n\tfound, err := d.Scanner.Scan(ctx, strings.NewReader(raw))\n\tif err != nil || len(found) > 0 {\n\t\tjb.log.Error(\"worker.fetched_withheld\", \"cause\", \"scan\")\n\t\treturn\n\t}\n\tjb.fetched = fs\n}\n\n// round decides one finished agent step: the role file, the diff guard and the\n// push. head is the commit the tests run on. done means the job has been\n// finished (or stopped) here; noCode means the step changed nothing to commit.\nfunc (jb *job) round(ctx context.Context, s step) (head string, noCode, done bool) {\n\tn := jb.is.Number\n\trf, ok := jb.peekRole(s.h)\n\tif !ok || rf.Status != closeout.StatusDone {\n\t\t// Close-out reports a missing, malformed or not-done result itself.\n\t\tjb.finishWithPR(ctx, s, \"\", closeout.Cause{})\n\t\treturn \"\", false, true\n\t}\n\tjb.setSummary(ctx, s.h, rf.Summary)\n\tjb.setFetched(ctx, s.h, rf.Fetched)\n\tstage, err := os.MkdirTemp(\"\", \"ard-code-\")\n\tif err == nil {\n\t\tdefer os.RemoveAll(stage)\n\t\t// The committer refuses a source path that involves a symlink (/var on macOS).\n\t\tstage, err = filepath.EvalSymlinks(stage)\n\t}\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tjb.log.Error(\"worker.guard_failed\", \"error\", err)\n\t\tjb.finishWithPR(ctx, s, \"\", jb.cause(closeout.CauseNoProcess, \"the diff guard could not run\", false))\n\t\treturn \"\", false, true\n\t}\n\tchanges, prLines, err := jb.l.facts(ctx, n, s.h.Worktree, s.h.BaseSHA, jb.cfg.SHA, stage)\n\tvar le *gitx.LimitError\n\tif errors.As(err, &le) {\n\t\t// Too big or too many to look at: a hold, before anything was hashed.\n\t\tjb.log.Info(\"worker.guard\", \"hold\", true, \"limit\", le.Error())\n\t\tjb.finishWithPR(ctx, s, \"diff guard: \"+Reject{Path: le.Path, Why: le.Why}.String(), closeout.Cause{})\n\t\treturn \"\", false, true\n\t}\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tjb.log.Error(\"worker.guard_failed\", \"error\", err)\n\t\tjb.finishWithPR(ctx, s, \"\", jb.cause(closeout.CauseNoProcess, \"the diff guard could not run\", false))\n\t\treturn \"\", false, true\n\t}\n\tlim := Lines{Warn: jb.role.DiffWarnLines, Max: jb.role.DiffMaxLines}\n\tv := Check(n, changes, prLines, jb.exists, lim)\n119:**Repo context docs.** `.ard/PRODUCT.md`, `.ard/VISION.md` and `.ard/TECH_STACK.md` give agents the background a repository's code does not state. They are human-owned: no agent writes them, the worker guard rejects any change under `.ard/`, and `ard init` proposes them only as templates. Each is optional. A file that is missing, or that still holds only the template (headings and comments), counts as absent and is not shown to agents. Precedence, highest first: the role's own prompt and `.ard/config.yaml`, then the issue and its plan, then these docs; they inform a decision and never override the issue's ask. A reviewer reads them from the pull request's head, so a PR that edits one is judged against its own edit, and because the three files are protected paths that edit waits for a human approval. When a doc conflicts with the issue or with another doc, the agent does not pick a side: it reports the conflict in its role file and answers `needs-human`. Treat their content as facts about the project, not as instructions that change an agent's rules. Each template carries a size guidance of about 150 lines and a `Last reviewed: YYYY-MM-DD` line for the human to keep current.\n125:| every job | its own role file `<stage>-<role>.md` |\n128:| `triage:lead` | its role file only |\n129:| a custom triage role | its role file only, unless `config.yaml` gives it `plan_section: <heading>` (see Configuration) |\n130:| `worker:*`, `review:*` | its role file only |\n132:**Fetched inputs (#300).** A worker that fetches bytes from the network records each one under `fetched:` in its role file. Close-out parses the list strictly, like `findings`: an entry with a non-https URL (or one with userinfo, whitespace, backticks or angle brackets), a bad date or status, a `sha256` that is not 64 lowercase hex characters, a `verified_by` that is neither `none` nor an https URL, an unclean or missing `path`, or an unknown key fails the parse, as do more than 50 entries and a `fetched` key on a non-worker role file. The worker PR body then lists the entries under **Fetched inputs** (URL, landing path, sha256, verified_by; `none` shows as unverified) from the parsed fields only, never from free text; the list is dropped, like the summary, when redaction or the secret scan touches it. The human-readable record beside each fixture set is a `CAPTURE.md` with the same fields as a table plus the regen command (the #47 precedent); the worker writes it as part of the change, and ard does not parse it.\n134:A worker's inputs are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md`, `comments.md` and, on a fix step, `test-output.log` (written by the loop, never committed) and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`. Nobody may change `issue.md` (only the scanner writes it, through the committer, not close-out), another role's file, any other file, or delete `plan.md`. A job that owns a `plan.md` section may add, replace in place, move or remove that section; a change to the text before the first `##` heading is allowed only when the job creates the file. Headings match exactly (case-sensitive), and `##` lines inside a fenced code block are text, not headings. A change outside the allowlist fails the job as `needs-human`: the comment names up to three offending paths, and only the redacted log is committed, never the offending files. A job with no valid role file still fails as `failed`, because the allowlist is checked after the role file parses. `logs/` is the controller's: files an agent writes there are ignored, not committed. `costs/` is the controller's too, but stricter: no role may write it, so an agent write there breaks the allowlist like any other path and fails the job as `needs-human`; the controller stages its own `costs/<job-id>.json` after that check, and it is never on `closeout.WritableFor`. The allowlist is code (`closeout.WritableFor`), not config; `plan_section` only names the heading a role owns. Workers and reviewers get their inputs outside `.ard/<issue#>/` (for example `.ard-input/<issue#>/`), because anything they write under it counts as their change. `issue.md` is the issue body without ard's cost block (see Guardrails), so the table never reaches an agent; the reviewer's untrusted `.ard/<issue#>/` input includes `costs/*.json`, which is controller-written and holds no free text.\n138:The stage in a role file's name is the label stage (`triage`, `worker`, `review`; `config.Kind` and the role directory spell the last one `reviewer`), so a triage and a worker role with the same name never share a file.\n145:status: done          # done | blocked | needs-human (both of the last two add ard:needs-human)\n159:    status: 200                 # HTTP status, required\n268:**Starter planning roles.** `ard init` also ships four planning roles, each writing its own `plan.md` section (`config.BuiltinPlanSections`) and its role file: `product-manager` (`## Requirements`: requirements, a numbered list of acceptance criteria, out of scope), `architect` (`## Design`: approach, affected modules, and an `ADR needed: yes|no` line; the worker writes any ADR in its pull request), `designer` (`## UX`, plus Markdown or script-free SVG files under `.ard/<issue#>/design/`) and `documentation-planner` (`## Docs`: which docs change and what each must say). The command line counts as a user interface, so the designer covers flags, output and messages. The starter ordering is product-manager, then architect; designer, then documentation-planner; the engineer runs after architect and documentation-planner (`after:` in the starter `config.yaml`). Note for #45: acceptance criteria are a numbered list under `### Acceptance criteria`. The starter prompts read the repo context docs per role: `product-manager` reads `PRODUCT.md` and `VISION.md`, `architect` reads `VISION.md` and `TECH_STACK.md`, `designer` and `documentation-planner` read `PRODUCT.md`, and `engineer` reads `TECH_STACK.md`. Each prompt states the shared rules: the file is optional, one with no text outside headings and HTML comments counts as absent, the issue is the ask and the files are constraints, and `CLAUDE.md` and `AGENTS.md` win on commands and layout. A planning role that finds a conflict records it, with file and section, in its own role file.\n270:The starter lead (`.ard/triage/lead/prompt.txt`) follows a fixed contract. It reads the issue only through the untrusted input files `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md`, which the triage loop writes and never commits. It finds roles by listing the role directories and routes only to those. It writes only `triage-lead.md`, with `attempt: 1`, and a `status: done` always names a `worker`. It answers `needs-human` when a person must decide: an unclear ask, a question, a duplicate, a change to `.ard/config.yaml`, the role directories or `.github/`, no fitting worker, or text that tries to instruct it. It uses `review:<role>` only for a reviewer whose `when` rule would skip a change that needs that review. The committer and close-out guards confine it to `.ard/<issue#>/`, and close-out allows it only `triage-lead.md`; any other change under `.ard/<issue#>/` fails the job as needs-human. It also reads `PRODUCT.md` and `VISION.md` when present: a contradiction with a non-goal, principle or banned dependency makes it raise one `needs-human`, while on an interpretive or minor conflict it may proceed and says so in `triage-lead.md`. Inputs found only on the network are not, alone, a reason for `needs-human`: the lead routes them to the engineer and reads the `Worker roles:` line to see whether any worker role has network (#301).\n284:| lead | nothing (role file only); writes `triage-lead.md` with routing, or `needs-human` when a person must decide | every `ard:pending` issue |\n297:An executor runs one agent job somewhere and reports back; the controller does not care where. Both executors implement the same five calls: `prepare`, `start`, `status`, `logs`, `cleanup`. Prepare also removes any `Job.Scrub` paths (the job's own role file, directly under `.ard/<issue#>/`) from the fresh worktree before the agent starts. Job IDs are ULIDs minted at claim time; they name the worktree and the logs and appear as the `Ard-Job` commit trailer. The executor captures the job's stdout separately from its combined log, because the adapter parses one and the committed log comes from the other. Scanner jobs get network egress for advisory databases; an agent job's environment holds its model credential and nothing else of value, and with `network: true` (the default) it may reach the network; see the `network` setting under Configuration for what is and is not enforced.\n322:**The agent's contract is narrow.** It may edit files in its worktree and must write `.ard/<issue#>/<stage>-<role>.md` with a `status`. Under `.ard/<issue#>/` it may change only its own role file and what its role's allowlist grants (see the `.ard/` contract). Exit 0 without that file counts as a failure, not a success, and so does a malformed file. The file counts only if this job wrote it: before the agent starts, the executor removes the job's role file from the worktree (`executor.Job.Scrub`), so a copy left from an earlier stage or run is never trusted. A role file the agent never wrote again fails the job (`role file not written by this job`, or `role file missing` when none was ever there). One it wrote again with identical bytes still counts, but only because the executor recorded the scrub in the handle (`Handle.Scrubbed`); close-out refuses an unscrubbed, unchanged role file (`role file not scrubbed by executor`). A non-zero exit fails the job even when a role file exists, and a failed job commits only its redacted log, never a partial `plan.md`.\n324:**Close-out is resumable.** The controller (`internal/closeout`) decides the outcome from the role file (only one this job wrote), stages the redacted files, then commits, requests the `next` labels, posts one summary comment (for a review with a verdict, one PR review instead; either is tagged `<!-- ard:closeout job=<id> -->` so a replay never posts twice) and finally swaps `:running` for `:done`, `:changes`, `ard:needs-human` or `ard:executor:failed`. It records its phase in local state after each step. `serve` resumes unfinished close-outs at startup, and the Reaper resumes, rather than fails, a job whose close-out has started. Three more outcomes exist for a job that ended without a usable result: `failed` (sets `ard:executor:failed`), `retry` (a named transient cause: no `ard:executor:failed`, the request label kept, the next claim is the automatic retry) and `usage-limit` (a stop at a subscription usage limit: `:running` is removed, nothing is added, the request label stays, and the issue is claimed again after the reset without using up the retry). All three commit only the redacted log. Their comment carries the reason, the last 50 log lines (cut from the redacted log, scanned again and capped at 8 KiB; if that scan flags or fails, the comment says the tail was withheld) and when the worktree and host files are kept until. `next` entries are applied only for roles that exist; a `review:<role>` the lead requests is applied when the worker opens the PR, not at triage close-out. Only changed files under `.ard/<issue#>/` that the job's allowlist permits are committed, so a newer `issue.md` on `main` is never overwritten.\n348:4. **Run the agent** with `.ard/workers/<role>/prompt.txt` and these untrusted inputs: `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md` (written by the loop, outside `.ard/<issue#>/`, never committed), `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md` when triage wrote one and `.ard/<issue#>/issue.md` when a scanner filed the issue (each only as a regular file, never through a symlink), the triage role files and logs, and, in a fix step, `.ard-input/<issue#>/test-output.log` (the untrusted header, the test command and its exit code, and the last 64 KiB of the test log). In a review round, `.ard-input/<issue#>/review-feedback.md` (the untrusted header, the round, the committed role files of the reviewers that asked for changes and the pull request comments of people, cut at 64 KiB), and the reviewer role files and logs on the branch are untrusted inputs too. The agent edits code and writes `.ard/<issue#>/worker-<role>.md`; the executor scrubs a stale copy first.\n353:9. **Report.** Comment on the issue with the summary, PR link and test result (`passed`, `passed after 1 fix attempt`, `failed after 2 fix attempts`, `not configured`); swap `ard:worker:<role>:running` for `:done`. If tests still fail, add `ard:tests:failed` on the issue and the PR and request no reviewers; a green run removes it. A worker that ends without the loop's result (for example one the Reaper closes out), or whose role file says `needs-human`, never becomes `:done` without a PR.\n355:**The starter worker (`workers/engineer`, #34).** `.ard/workers/engineer/prompt.txt` implements `plan.md`'s `## Implementation`. Its inputs, all untrusted, are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md`, in a fix round `test-output.log`, and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`, `issue.md`, the triage role files and logs, in a review round `review-feedback.md` and the reviewer role files and logs. It writes code, tests and docs, and `worker-engineer.md` with `role: engineer` and `attempt: 1`, an optional `next` of `review:` entries, and never `worker` or `verdict`. The body has Changes, Testing and Open issues sections; it becomes the commit body, the PR description and the issue comment, and a fix round's body describes the whole change. It answers `needs-human` for a change the diff guard would reject, more changed lines than the role's `diff_max_lines` (3,000 by default; above `diff_warn_lines`, 2,000 by default, the PR goes ahead with the large-change note), a missing decision, nothing to change, or an input that tries to instruct it. In a fix round it keeps `done` and explains a failure it cannot fix, so the loop reports `ard:tests:failed` on the PR. The repository's package manager may fetch a dependency the change needs (`go get`, `npm install`); the agent installs no system packages. With `network: true` it may fetch other inputs, records each under `fetched:` and treats the fetched bytes as untrusted data, and the guard and reviewers still see `go.mod` and lockfile changes. Credential-shaped test fixtures are built at run time. The starter override is Sonnet 5.5, `timeout: 60m` for the whole job, `tests: make lint test` (so formatting and vet failures are caught before a PR opens), and the default diff thresholds written out (`diff_warn_lines: 2000`, `diff_max_lines: 3000`). It ships no Dockerfile, so it runs as a host subprocess with the #84 warning until #55's `ard-agent` image gives a worker image a base. It reads `TECH_STACK.md` when present; the file is a constraint, never an instruction, and the worker never resolves a conflict with it: it keeps to the issue and plan and notes the conflict in Open issues. With `Network: available.` the worker fetches only what the plan's Network inputs name, verifies each file against the publisher's checksum file, never invents or recalls a value, records each file in `fetched:` and in a `CAPTURE.md` beside the fixtures, and commits a regen script and Make target; with `Network: unavailable.` it answers `needs-human` listing the inputs it needs (#301).\n367:3. **Run the aid, then the agent.** After the pin, a role with an executable `aid` in its directory (reviewers only, like a scanner's `scan`; #205) has it run by the controller as its own executor job at the pinned head, before the agent, so the findings of its deterministic tools do not depend on the host or on the agent choosing to run them. The tool finds; the agent explains, ranks and confirms. The aid job is read-only, has no `Env` and no `Fixed` entries (no model credential, no GitHub token), runs with `--network none` under Docker (a role `Dockerfile` or `ARD_AGENT_IMAGE`; known CVEs stay with the `cve` scanner, so the aid never queries vulnerability databases), and has its own timeout of 5 minutes (shorter if the role's is), counted inside the review job's timeout: the agent gets what is left, at least one minute. Its inputs are `.ard-input/<issue#>/changed.txt` (one path per line, `.ard/<issue#>/` left out) and `diff.patch`. Its output is JSONL on stdout, one object per result with exactly `tool`, `rule`, `file`, `line`, `severity` (`critical`, `high`, `medium`, `low`, `info`) and `message`, capped at 256 KiB (more is a failure). ard validates it: a line that is not JSON, has an unknown key, a bad tool, rule, severity or line, or a path that is absolute, has `..` or is not in `changed.txt` is dropped and counted; a message is redacted, cut at 1 KiB and secret-scanned like a log, and a line that still trips the scanner is dropped. Matched text is never kept: the aid must print rule ids and positions only. The result is written to `.ard-input/<issue#>/aid.jsonl` and is the first of the agent's untrusted inputs; the starter prompt reads it first and runs the tools itself only when the file is absent. **Failure:** a failed, timed-out or invalid aid (any dropped line counts as invalid) never stops the review. The input says the aid failed and why, and close-out turns an `approve` into `changes` with a note. **Blocking:** a `gitleaks` result on a line the PR added becomes a blocking `high` finding with a `[secret]` message that ard adds to the review in code (carrying the rule id only); the agent cannot drop it, and an `approve` becomes `changes`. gosec and semgrep results stay advisory. Both rules apply only to roles that have an `aid`. **Host mode:** with no role `Dockerfile` and no `ARD_AGENT_IMAGE`, the aid runs on the host, as ard-owned code that reads but never executes PR code; its environment holds no credential, but nothing enforces its network, and it runs as the controller's uid without a sandbox: the residual risk of #84 applies to it too. The starter `security-review/aid` pins gosec, gitleaks and semgrep (vendored local rules, `--metrics=off`, `--disable-nosem`) in the role's `Dockerfile`, built on the default ard-agent image (k8s role images stay with #245). Then **run the agent** with `.ard/reviewers/<role>/prompt.txt` and these untrusted inputs: `.ard-input/<issue#>/diff.patch` (the controller's diff from the merge base with `main` to the pinned head, `--stat` then the patch, taken with `--text`, `--no-textconv` and attributes read from the empty tree so a `.gitattributes` entry cannot hide a change, with this issue's `.ard/<issue#>/` left out and cut at 1 MiB), `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md` (written by the loop, never committed), the committed `.ard/<issue#>/` (plan, triage and worker role files and logs) and the sidecar (security policy, license allowlist). The loop runs every git command against the controller clone, never in the reviewer's worktree (the agent itself may run read-only git there). In a later round it also gets `.ard-input/<issue#>/previous-review.md`, its own earlier report, read from the pinned head, and says which findings are resolved.\n497:Scanners also take `route` (`pending`, `none` or `needs-human`). Anything unset takes its default: intake in `label` mode with `ard:pending`, scanners every 4 h with `max_open` 5 and `group_size` 10, triage every 15 min, a 30-minute job timeout, `make test`, 2 test-fix attempts, 3 review rounds and worker diff thresholds of 2,000 lines (warn) and 3,000 lines (hold). Durations accept `m`, `h` and `d`. A sidecar `repo` must be `owner/name` and its `ref` a valid git ref name (no `:`, leading `-`, `..` or spaces; an abbreviated SHA is rejected, use all 40 characters); `ard config check` reports the file and key. A worker's `timeout` and `max_budget_usd` cover the whole job (every step), and `tests: \"\"` disables its test step. Per-role keys are `every`, `max_open`, `group_size`, `autoclose`, `timeout`, `agent`, `model`, `max_budget_usd`, `budget_usd_per_day` (that role's own daily cap in USD; unset or 0 means only the repo cap applies), `sidecar`, `after`, `plan_section`, `tests`, `max_test_fixes`, `max_review_rounds`, `diff_warn_lines` and `diff_max_lines` (worker roles; positive integers, warn no more than max; above warn the PR proceeds with a note, above max it is held), `reviewers` and `when` (`paths`, or `triage` roles that ran; at least one is required). `ard config check` warns when every reviewer has a `when:` rule, because a PR matching none gets no review and stays a draft. `plan_section` is for triage roles: the `## <heading>` of `plan.md` the role owns (one line, no leading `#`, unique across triage roles). The starter roles default to Requirements (product-manager), Design (architect), UX (designer), Docs (documentation-planner) and Implementation (engineer), and a value in `config.yaml` overrides that; a triage role with none may write only its own role file. There are no per-role config files: `ard init` writes a role's starting overrides into `config.yaml` for the roles it ships. Once a second repo adopts ard, role directories can move to a shared roles repo pinned by ref, like a sidecar; a repo's own directory of the same name wins. `after` names triage roles, with no self-reference and no cycles.\n524:- **Agents write only their own files.** Close-out lets a job change under `.ard/<issue#>/` only its own role file and what its role owns (a `plan.md` section, `design/`); anything else fails the job as `needs-human` and is not committed (see the `.ard/` contract).\n530:- **Committed output is redacted.** Close-out strips known secret values, and replaces the controller host's paths (see \"All agent output is committed too\") with placeholders, in each log and every `.ard/<issue#>/` file it commits, and runs a secret scan before anything is committed or posted. A hit on a log keeps that log host-local and adds `ard:needs-human`; a hit on a role file or `plan.md` commits only the clean log and adds `ard:needs-human`. Comments never quote text the scan flagged (and the same scan runs on worker code before it is pushed, see above, and the worker's PR body and code commit message get the same secret and host-path replacement; the config-load error posted to the status issue gets the host-path replacement too), never forge an `ard:` marker and never `@`-mention anyone. The scan fails closed: if it cannot run, nothing is committed. An optional second pass runs a pinned gitleaks binary (`redact.gitleaks`, see \"All agent output is committed too\"); it is off by default, fails closed, and a hit is handled like an internal hit. Once a log's redacted copy is committed, close-out deletes the raw host log; if redaction failed or the scan flagged it, the raw log is kept (and the keep logged) for a human to inspect.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01934B8ztwx34bUwHE8LvQnL","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
